--TEST--
`Core\Request::files` names the walk rather than taking it, so a program answering no request is refused where it asked and not at the first part — spec § 15, ADR 0105 §§ 1-2, ADR 0012 § 7
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Where the refusal lands is the question, and it is one only a lazy member can
// get wrong: a `foreach` drives `iterate()`, `advance()` and `current()` after
// the member has already answered, so a `files` that deferred its check to the
// first part would hand a program a walk it could never take. The loop body
// below is unreachable either way -- what the case pins is that nothing inside
// it ran, and that the sentence names `files` and not one of the three names a
// `foreach` drives.
//
// The body also spells the whole of what ADR 0105 § 2 says a part carries: the
// form field it arrived under, the name the client claimed for it, and the type
// the client declared. There is no fourth reader and there is deliberately no
// `size` -- § 2 refuses one, because there is no honest value to put there
// before the part has been consumed.
try {
    foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
        echo $part->name(), " ", $part->filename(), " ", $part->contentType(), "\n";
    }
    echo "the walk ended without a refusal\n";
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Request::files(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
